    <!-- PART 1 -->
    <section class="part" id="part-problem" aria-labelledby="h-problem">
      <div class="part-eyebrow"><i data-lucide="alert-circle" width="13" height="13" aria-hidden="true"></i>Part 1</div>
      <h2 id="h-problem">The Problem Vinifera Solves</h2>
      <p>Every winery that sells wine directly to consumers through a club membership faces the same operational headache. Members sign up, pay a recurring fee, and receive curated wine shipments several times a year. The winery must manage who those members are, what they bought, where they live, when their next shipment goes out, whether their credit card on file works, and why some of them quietly disappear after eighteen months.</p>
      <p>Most wineries today manage this through legacy software built a decade ago — software designed for the era before smartphones, before machine learning, and before customers expected the same seamless digital experience from their wine club that they get from Netflix or Amazon. The result is operational friction that costs wineries real money.</p>
      <div class="callout callout-wine">
        <p><strong>The documented failure pattern:</strong> Billing errors. Shipments to outdated addresses. Members who intended to cancel but would have stayed if someone had reached out at the right moment. A member portal so difficult to use that members call the tasting room instead of helping themselves.</p>
      </div>
      <p>The market has known about these problems for years. A handful of established platforms — Commerce7, WineDirect, OrderPort, vinSUITE — have built real businesses serving this need. Some of them are good. None has solved the full problem. Commerce7, the current market leader, launched an AI churn prediction feature in June 2026. It is a start. But it lives in a separate dashboard, requires a separate login, and does not connect to the communications or loyalty tools operators need to act on what the AI tells them.</p>
      <p>That gap — the distance between knowing a member is at risk and having the tools to save them, all in a single place — is what Vinifera was built to close.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- PART 2 -->
    <section class="part" id="part-what" aria-labelledby="h-what">
      <div class="part-eyebrow"><i data-lucide="layers" width="13" height="13" aria-hidden="true"></i>Part 2</div>
      <h2 id="h-what">What Vinifera Is</h2>
      <p>Vinifera is a web-based platform for wine club management. It targets small to mid-size wineries — producers making between 500 and 5,000 cases per year, selling directly to consumers through a recurring membership program.</p>
      <p>The name comes from <em>Vitis vinifera</em>, the Latin species name for the primary wine grape vine. It signals domain knowledge to winery operators and reads as a premium brand word, without the overused "wine" prefix that defines most platform names in this market.</p>
      <p>At its core, Vinifera is two things delivered as one product.</p>
      <div class="tech-grid">
        <div class="tech-card">
          <div class="tech-card-label">For Staff</div>
          <h4>Administration Portal</h4>
          <p>The tool winery staff use every day. Handles member records, shipment processing, billing, club tier configuration, email communications, compliance, and analytics — all in one interface.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="tech-card">
          <div class="tech-card-label">For Members</div>
          <h4>Member Portal</h4>
          <p>The self-service interface wine club members see. Preview shipments, update addresses, swap bottles, pause membership, update payment — with magic-link login. No password required.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="callout">
        <p><strong>Why magic link?</strong> The documented failure mode on competing platforms is that their member portals work on paper and fail in practice — members forget passwords, revert to phone calls. Vinifera eliminates passwords entirely: enter your email, receive a secure one-click link, and you are in.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

